just last month angular 5 was released  and with it comes the new serviceworker  module that makes building progressive  web apps easier than ever before all we  need to do is set a few configuration  variables and angular will automatically  generate a serviceworker with our  production build in this video I'm going  to show you how to use the worker to  score 100 on a progressive web app on it  and I'll show you how to customize the  worker to fit the needs of your specific  application I'm going to do this by  creating my own version of hacker news  which is the gold standard for comparing  performance and usability across various  JavaScript frameworks if you're just  finding me for the first time make sure  to subscribe and for this video I have a  full working demo deployed to firebase  hosting feel free to mess around with it  and if you see anything you want to  optimize our fix send me a pull request  on github  before we get going I just want to point  out that service workers aren't  supported on all modern browsers Safari  and Microsoft edge or the main holdouts  but they should be supported in the  future at the time of this video the  serviceworker is only available in that  beta version of the angular CLI so  you'll want to install it with the next  tag and then make sure the version you  have installed is 1.6 or greater and if  you're starting from an existing angular  5 app you can simply create the files  I'm about to show you in the upcoming  steps here to create a new app with the  serviceworker configured automatically  run ng new with the serviceworker flag  then inside that new app the first place  I'm going to go is the angular CLI JSON  file inside here you'll want to add  manifest.json to the assets array we're  going to create this file manually here  in the next step then down here you'll  notice that the serviceworker option is  set to true which tells angular to  generate the serviceworker at Build time  from there you're going to create a web  app manifest in the source directory the  purpose of the manifest is to tell  browsers how to handle your web app  we're just going to set the bare minimum  options for now so we have a start URL  which is just the root and then you'll  need a theme color and background color  then you'll need to add some icons if  you want your app to be installable on  mobile devices the bare minimum is just  a 512 by 512 image but you can add  additional sizes here if you want to as  well you will want to save your images  inside the assets folder in your angular  app now really quickly I want to show  you how the serviceworker gets  registered inside the app module it will  mr. the service worker in the ng SW  worker file if the build is production  however this didn't work for me using  angularfire too so I want to show you an  alternative way to register a worker so  you can go into the main TS file then  after your applications bootstrap you  can see if the service worker is in the  browser navigator and then register your  service worker there now we're ready to  move on to the important part which is  the ng Service Worker config file this  file tells angular how to generate the  Service Worker so I want to go through  here and explain to you what all these  different options mean the first option  is index which is just the entry point  for your app and then we have an assets  group array which defines the static  assets that are going to be cached by  the Service Worker  the first asset group is called app and  the install mode is prefetch prefetch  means that the worker is going to go out  and cache all these assets right away so  essentially these are going to be all  the files that are necessary to run your  app offline so you'll notice it has all  the files that are known at build time  including our index.html entry point as  well as the various bundle files we can  also add additional external files which  is probably going to be necessary if  you're using something like angular  material you can do that by adding an  additional urls array and add the URLs  that you want to cache to it this would  be a good place for web fonts or any  other styles you get from a CDN now I'm  going to jump down to our next asset  group which this time is installed with  the Lazy strategy we're going to add the  assets directory to it and the only  difference is that it will cache these  assets only when they're actually  requested by the user in some way  through the app at this point our worker  knows how to cache all of our assets  that are known at Build time but what  happens if we receive data from an  external API for that we can use the  data groups array which has two distinct  strategies the first strategy we're  looking at here is API freshness this  strategy will always try to make a  network call for the latest and greatest  data unless it times out after 10  seconds in this case then it will  default to whatever's in the cache for  our hacker news app we're only going to  use this strategy when the user  navigates to the new URL which contains  the latest posts from hacker news you  can add your own URLs to the urls array  and then you can control the cache  behavior in that cache config object so  freshness is great when data is  constantly changing but if it doesn't  change very often  you can use the API performance strategy  the difference here is it's going to try  and use the data in the cache but if the  max age of the cache has expired then it  will go out and make another Network  call for new data data served from the  cache is going to be a lot more  performant and appear instantaneous in  the eyes of the user eventually when you  run a production build you can go into  Chrome and pull up the network tab and  you'll see that assets are served from  the serviceworker an asset from a  serviceworker only takes a few  milliseconds so it will appear  instantaneous for your users before  getting to that point though we first  need to go into the index.html file and  add a few meta tags to ensure that we  get the 100 pwace core the main things  here that we link the manifest.json file  and that we add a meta tag for our theme  color the index.html file can be thought  of as the splash page similar to the  initial loading page on a native mobile  app the typical way to handle this page  is with your app icon and possibly a  very small amount of text then the last  thing we'll do is add a no script tag to  let the user know that they need Java  Script enabled to use this app at this  point we should be able to build our app  by running ng build flag production  after that's finished we can go into the  dist folder and you'll notice a couple  extra files here we have manifest.json  and NGS W JSON which contains all of our  actual compiled configuration for the  worker if we open that up you can see  that it lists all of our version bundle  files as well as any other configuration  that we set in that main configuration  file for the worker then if we go into  the worker JavaScript file we can see  all kinds of crazy JavaScript that were  really glad we didn't have to write from  scratch when you're ready to test your  app you can go into Chrome into the  audit tab and then click perform an  audit it's going to simulate how your  app would load on a slower 3G connection  on a mobile device for a progressive web  app it's going to verify that you  register a serviceworker it's also going  to look at your manifest file as well as  a handful of other related audits  currently we're at a hundred score for  progressive web app but our performance  is only at 79 it measures performance  based on how quickly you can get the  first meaningful content on the page and  then how long it takes your page to  become interactive by the user  seventy-nine isn't a very good score  compared to other hacker news PWA s so  in the next video i'll show you how to  optimize angular to bring the score up  into the 90s if you want to start  building your own hacker news app you  can actually do it using angular fire 2  because the hacker news API is hosted on  firebase so you can achieve that by  simply installing angular fire - and  then when you initialize your app you  use that database URL that is the hacker  news endpoint if you want to build the  exact app I built here go to angular  firebase comm and I have greater detail  of the actual app source code there  that's it for the angular 5 Service  Worker module this video helped you  please like and subscribe and if you  want to take your PWA to the next level  consider becoming a pro member at  angular firebase comm you'll get a free  copy of my book as well as a whole bunch  of exclusive content thanks for watching  and I'll see you next time  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]
 

